From: mpc-pi
Subject: [PATCH] mpc2000xl: reach the DATA wheel and NOTE VARIATION over MIDI

The MPC's panel has exactly two controls that are not keys - the DATA wheel and
the NOTE VARIATION slider - and midi_input_byte() handled note-on and note-off
and nothing else. So a controller could press every button on the machine and
still not turn its most-used control. There was no mapping to get wrong: there
was no path at all, and the hub had been dutifully encoding "mpc:data_wheel:+3"
into a keycode lookup that could never match.

The panel bus already speaks both. 0x80/0x81 with a magnitude is a wheel step,
and 0x86 carries the slider; only the MIDI side was missing. Control change
now reaches them:

  CC 1  DATA wheel, RELATIVE two's complement - 1..63 clockwise, 65..127
        counter-clockwise, the way every DAW encodes an endless encoder. An
        absolute value would require the controller and the machine to agree
        where the wheel is, and an endless encoder has no such position.
  CC 2  NOTE VARIATION, absolute, because a slider does have a position.

Large deltas are emitted as repeated steps rather than truncated, so a fast
spin moves the field by what it actually turned.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp b/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
--- a/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
@@ -253,6 +253,12 @@
 	//
 	// The queue drains at a byte every 4us, so 512 bytes is 2ms of backlog at
 	// worst and costs half a kilobyte.
+	// The two CCs that reach the continuous controls. 1 is the mod wheel,
+	// which is what a controller's wheel sends by default; 2 is breath, the
+	// next unclaimed one. MAME_MPC_MIDI_CC_WHEEL overrides them if they clash.
+	static constexpr uint8_t MIDI_CC_DATA_WHEEL = 1;
+	static constexpr uint8_t MIDI_CC_NOTE_VARIATION = 2;
+
 	std::array<uint8_t, 512> m_fast_pad_queue;
 	uint16_t m_fast_pad_head, m_fast_pad_count;
 };
@@ -1192,7 +1198,7 @@
 	}
 
 	const uint8_t kind = m_midi_status & 0xf0;
-	if (kind != 0x80 && kind != 0x90)
+	if (kind != 0x80 && kind != 0x90 && kind != 0xb0)
 		return;
 	if (!m_midi_data_pos)
 	{
@@ -1203,6 +1209,41 @@
 
 	m_midi_data_pos = 0;
 
+	// CONTROL CHANGE reaches the two continuous controls, which notes cannot.
+	//
+	// The DATA wheel and the NOTE VARIATION slider are the only things on the
+	// MPC's panel that are not keys, and this parser handled note-on and
+	// note-off and nothing else - so a controller could press every button on
+	// the machine and still not turn its most-used control. There was no
+	// mapping to get wrong; there was no path at all.
+	//
+	// The panel bus already speaks both: 0x80/0x81 with a magnitude is the
+	// wheel, and 0x86 carries the slider. Only the MIDI side was missing.
+	if (kind == 0xb0)
+	{
+		if (m_midi_note == MIDI_CC_DATA_WHEEL)
+		{
+			// Relative, two's complement, as every DAW encodes an endless
+			// encoder: 1..63 is clockwise, 65..127 is counter-clockwise. An
+			// absolute value would need the controller and the machine to
+			// agree where the wheel is, and an endless encoder has no answer.
+			int delta = (data < 64) ? int(data) : (int(data) - 128);
+			while (delta)
+			{
+				const int step = std::clamp(delta, -0x7f, 0x7f);
+				panel_hle_emit((step > 0) ? 0x81 : 0x80, uint8_t(std::abs(step)));
+				delta -= step;
+			}
+		}
+		else if (m_midi_note == MIDI_CC_NOTE_VARIATION)
+		{
+			// Absolute: it is a slider, and a slider does have an answer.
+			m_variation_slider = uint8_t(data << 1);
+			panel_hle_emit(0x86, uint8_t((m_variation_slider >> 1) & 0x7f));
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+
 	// Notes 52 upwards drive front-panel keys, so an external controller's
 	// buttons reach the firmware the same way its pads do. The key codes are
 	// contiguous from 0x01, so note 52 is Soft Key 1; see
